<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>Two pipeline behaviors that put a cache around a query and evict it after the command that changed the data commits.

A query implementing ICacheableQuery&lt;TValue&gt; supplies its own key and policy, and is served from cache without the handler knowing a cache exists. Only the value of a successful Result&lt;TValue&gt; is cached, so entries round-trip through the distributed cache's JSON serializer. A failed Result is never cached, so a transient NotFound is not pinned for the whole expiry window. A command implementing IInvalidatesCache declares the keys and tags it makes stale, and the eviction is deferred through ICommandScope until the outermost command's transaction has committed — so a concurrent reader can never repopulate the cache from pre-commit state, and a failed command evicts nothing. When an IRequestContext carrying a tenant is registered, keys and tags are prefixed per tenant, so one tenant can neither read nor invalidate another's entries.

These behaviors are a separate package because SharedKernel.Application.Behaviors will not take a cache dependency on behalf of services that never cache anything. Register them into the same pipeline with AddCachingBehaviors(). Depends on SharedKernel.Application.Behaviors and SharedKernel.Caching.Abstractions.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>First release.

WHAT IS IN IT
- CachingBehavior&lt;TRequest,TResponse&gt; over ICacheableQuery&lt;TValue&gt;, in the Query stage.
- CacheInvalidationBehavior&lt;TRequest,TResponse&gt; over IInvalidatesCache, in the Command stage.
- AddCachingBehaviors(), which registers both in their correct stages and makes Build() fail at startup when no ICacheService is registered.

THINGS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU ADOPT IT
- The cache stores TValue, never the Result, and a hit rebuilds Result&lt;TValue&gt;.Success(value). TValue must round-trip through System.Text.Json; add it to the cache's SerializerContext if the service registers one.
- Stampede-protected: the handler runs inside one ICacheService.GetOrSetAsync call. A failed Result is returned and never cached, so a query waiting on the same key runs the handler itself. Eager refresh and factory timeouts are switched off so the handler never runs outside the request.
- ICacheableQuery&lt;OrderDto&gt; is also an IQuery&lt;OrderDto&gt;, so a query declares it alone.
- The cache key must include everything the result depends on. Tenant scoping is applied automatically when a tenant is present, but it is not user scoping.
- Eviction runs only when the command succeeded and the outermost command committed. A failed or throwing handler evicts nothing, because nothing was written.
- SharedKernel.Analyzers' SK0017 and SK0018 flag a command implementing the query marker, and a query implementing the command marker.

See the package README for the invalidation-ordering diagram and the full reference.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageTags>cqrs;mediatr;pipeline-behavior;caching;cache-invalidation;shared-kernel</PackageTags>
